#
# NOTE ON MODULE LOADING AND NAMESPACES
# -------------------------------------
# Each lib/*.za file begins with `namespace main`. This forces all functions
# from every module into the main:: namespace, bypassing the USE chain.
#
# Why not USE + the modules normally?
# The USE chain performs a linear scan (with locks) across every namespace
# on every bare-name function/global lookup. With 7 modules, that added
# 20-30 FPS of overhead — every per-frame call to draw_scene, update_particles,
# etc. was hitting 7 string comparisons and map lookups before resolving.
#
# This is NOT the recommended za pattern. The intended design is:
#   module "lib/foo.za" as foo
#   use +foo
# and then call foo::my_function() or rely on the chain for bare names.
# However, for performance-critical code where functions are called thousands
# of times per frame, the USE chain overhead is measurable. If you are
# writing a non-interactive script or a UI tool, use the normal USE pattern.
# If you are writing a real-time renderer and see FPS drop after splitting
# into modules, the `namespace main` trick is a valid escape hatch.
#
# Tradeoff: all function names across all modules must be unique.
#

# 3D Demo - VBO Batched Vertex Version

doc '
3D Town Tech Demo for the Za Scripting Language
================================================

This is a proof-of-concept 3D game written entirely in za. It demonstrates:
- OpenGL fixed-function and shader-based rendering
- VBO-based static and dynamic geometry
- Skeletal animation via C library (libskel.so)
- Frustum culling, particle systems, day/night cycle, UI panels, minimap radar

PERFORMANCE NOTICE
------------------
This is a reference implementation, not an optimized game engine. FPS varies
widely depending on scene complexity and culling. It exists primarily to show
what za can do, not to compete.

HARDWARE & SYSTEM DEPENDENCIES
------------------------------
Requires OpenGL 3.3+ Compatibility Profile. May not run on systems without
Mesa/AMD/NVIDIA proprietary drivers or on macOS.

The following system libraries and headers must be pre-installed:
  - libglfw.so.3          (/usr/include/GLFW/glfw3.h)
  - libGL.so.1            (/usr/include/GL/gl.h)
  - libX11.so.6           (/usr/include/X11/Xlib.h)
  - libwayland-client.so.0  (/usr/include/wayland-client.h)
  - libpng16.so
  - libgd-dev (for building libtextrender.so from textrender.c)

System fonts used (distribution-specific fallbacks in script):
  - /usr/share/fonts/Adwaita/AdwaitaSans-Regular.ttf
  - /usr/share/fonts/truetype/liberation/LiberationMono-Regular.ttf

The project also requires two custom shared libraries that must be built
from source in this directory:
  - libtextrender.so  (make -f Makefile)
  - libskel.so        (see MODELS.md for build instructions)

MODULAR STRUCTURE
-----------------
The script is split into modules under lib/ to keep the codebase manageable.
All global variables and constants live in this main file; modules contain
only functions. Each module starts with "namespace main" to bypass the USE
chain overhead — see the comment below the module imports for details.

